            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: Are there direct flights from Seattle to Paris? </h3>
            <p>Yes, Delta operates year-round nonstop service from Seattle to Paris CDG, typically daily during peak season and 4-5 weekly in winter. Air France offers seasonal nonstop service May through October. </p>

            <h3>Q2: What is the cheapest time to fly from Seattle to Paris? </h3>
            <p>November and January-February offer the lowest fares, with economy round-trips often at $550-$950. Summer commands premium pricing. Book 45-60 days ahead for peak season. </p>

            <h3>Q3: Do US citizens need a visa for France? </h3>
            <p>No visa required for tourism under 90 days within any 180-day Schengen period. A valid passport with three months of remaining validity suffices. </p>

            <h3>Q4: How long is the flight from Seattle to Paris? </h3>
            <p>Nonstop flights take approximately 9 hours 20 minutes to 10 hours 10 minutes. Connecting itineraries range from 12 to 17 hours depending on the layover. </p>

            <h3>Q5: How do I get from Charles de Gaulle airport to central Paris? </h3>
            <p>RER B train reaches central Paris in 35-40 minutes for approximately €12. RoissyBus serves Opéra in 60-75 minutes for €14. Taxis charge a €55-€70 fixed rate to central Paris. </p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Paris? </h3>
            <p>The Euro is France's currency. US dollars are not accepted. Credit cards work universally; chip-and-PIN preferred. ATMs plentiful. Notify bank before travel. </p>

            <h3>Q7: What should I pack for Paris from Seattle? </h3>
            <p>Comfortable walking shoes, layers for temperature swings, smart casual attire for evening dining, umbrella, and universal power adapter. Paris weather is milder than Seattle in winter but can be similarly unpredictable. </p>

            <h3>Q8: Is Paris safe for American tourists? </h3>
            <p>Paris is generally safe. Standard precautions regarding pickpockets in tourist zones and on the Metro-violent crime against tourists is rare. The emergency number is 112. </p>

            <h3>Q9: What is the best way to avoid jet lag flying Seattle to Paris? </h3>
            <p>The nine-hour time difference is significant. Choose afternoon/evening departures arriving in Paris in the morning. Sleep on the flight. Expose yourself to daylight immediately upon arrival. Stay awake until local evening. Hydrate throughout. </p>

            <h3>Q10: Can I visit other French cities easily from Paris? </h3>
            <p>Yes. TGV high-speed rail connects Paris to Lyon (2 hours), Bordeaux (2 hours), Marseille (3 hours), and Strasbourg (1h 45m). Domestic flights available to Nice, Toulouse, and regional airports. </p>







